var boss={title:'The Review Council',blurb:'A teammate hands you code that does not work. Choose how you would handle it.',intro:'Code has arrived that does not behave. The Council asks for five choices about how you would find and fix problems.',
steps:[
{q:'You run some code and it stops with a long error message. What do you do first?',opts:[{t:'Read the last line for the kind of error and what it says, then look at the line number it names',pts:2,why:'The last line and the line number point straight at the problem in most cases.'},{t:'Delete the code and start again from scratch',pts:0,why:'You throw away information. The message told you what was wrong.'},{t:'Paste a random fix from the internet and see if it works',pts:1,why:'Sometimes it works, but without understanding the error you cannot tell if it fixed the cause or hid it.'}]},
{q:'A function runs without errors but gives the wrong answer for one input. What is the best next step?',opts:[{t:'Shrink the input to the smallest case that is still wrong, work out the right answer by hand, and print values inside the function to see where they part ways',pts:2,why:'A small case you can check by hand, plus printing, turns guessing into looking.'},{t:'Try changing several lines at once until the answer looks right',pts:0,why:'You will not know which change mattered, and you may add new bugs.'},{t:'Assume the test is wrong and move on',pts:0,why:'Possible, but you should check by hand before deciding.'}]},
{q:'Your function prints the right number on screen, but the code that calls it receives None. What is the likely cause?',opts:[{t:'The function uses print but never return, so nothing is handed back',pts:2,why:'print shows a value; return gives it back. A function with no return gives back None.'},{t:'The number is too large for Python to return',pts:0,why:'Python handles very large whole numbers, so this is not the problem.'},{t:'The caller needs to be indented further',pts:1,why:'Indentation mistakes cause errors, but this symptom is a missing return.'}]},
{q:'You write a helper that shuffles or sorts a list, but the caller\'s original list ends up changed. How do you prevent that?',opts:[{t:'Work on a copy (list(items) or sorted(items)) and return the copy',pts:2,why:'Copying first leaves the caller\'s list alone. A plain b = a only gives the same list a second name.'},{t:'Tell the caller not to look at the original list',pts:0,why:'That is not a fix; the function should be safe to call.'},{t:'Use .sort() on the original because it is faster',pts:0,why:'.sort() changes the list in place, which is exactly the problem.'}]},
{q:'How do you decide whether a function you wrote (or an AI wrote) can be trusted?',opts:[{t:'Run it on several inputs where you know the answer, including edge cases like an empty list, one item, negatives and ties, and keep those as asserts',pts:2,why:'Edge cases are where the bugs hide, and kept asserts stop old bugs coming back.'},{t:'Run it once on a normal input; if it works it is fine',pts:1,why:'One normal input proves very little.'},{t:'Trust it if the code looks neat',pts:0,why:'Neat code can still be wrong. Only running it tells you.'}]}]};
